<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP Dependency Check suppressions.

  RULES FOR THIS FILE — read before adding an entry:

  1. A suppression is a statement that a REPORTED vulnerability does not apply
     to this project, OR that it applies but has no published fix yet. It is
     not a way to make a red build green. If a fixed release EXISTS, upgrade
     the dependency instead — always. (Worked example: CVE-2026-65898, the
     DOMPurify flaw in swagger-ui, is NOT in this file. Swagger UI is served to
     real browsers, so it was fixed by raising springdoc 2.8.6 -> 2.8.17.)

  2. Every entry MUST carry:
       - the CVE id,
       - a <notes> explaining WHY it is suppressed (which code path is absent,
         or why no upgrade is available),
       - an expiry date in the notes, no more than 90 days out.

  3. An undated suppression is a permanently disabled gate. Re-review on expiry;
     if the CVE still has no fix, re-date it deliberately rather than leaving it
     open forever.

  4. NEVER raise failBuildOnCVSS in pom.xml to clear a finding. That disables
     the gate for every dependency, forever, invisibly. This file at least makes
     each exemption reviewable in a diff.

  5. "The version range looks wrong" is NOT grounds for suppression on its own.
     A cautionary tale from this project: the Tomcat findings were initially
     read as CPE range artifacts because the advisories also list an 11.x
     branch. Once an NVD API key was configured, the gate printed the matched
     ranges and they were unambiguous — >=10.1.0 <10.1.58 genuinely covered the
     shipped version. They were REAL vulnerabilities. Some were then FIXED by
     raising tomcat.version; only those with no published fix are suppressed.

  6. SCOPE A packageUrl TO EVERY ARTIFACT THE CVE IS REPORTED AGAINST.
     Learned the hard way: the Tomcat entries below were first scoped to
     `tomcat-embed-core` alone. spring-boot-starter-web pulls BOTH
     tomcat-embed-core AND tomcat-embed-websocket from the same source tree, and
     Dependency Check reports the identical CVEs against both jars — so the
     gate stayed red on the websocket jar while the core jar was suppressed.
     The gate step now prints the list of artifacts each finding was reported
     against, precisely so this can be got right in one pass. Scope to the
     narrowest pattern that covers all of them, never to a whole group "just in
     case".

  7. "COMPONENT ABSENT" MEANS THE VULNERABLE CLASSES ARE NOT ON THE CLASSPATH —
     verify it, do not assume it from the artifact name. A CPE match at the
     PRODUCT level ("apache log4j") does not mean the vulnerable MODULE is
     present: log4j-api is a facade jar; the flaws below live in log4j-core.
     Each category-A entry states what was checked and how.

  TWO CATEGORIES OF ENTRY EXIST HERE — they are not equivalent:
    (A) VULNERABLE COMPONENT ABSENT — the affected module is not on the
        classpath at all. Low risk, verified by inspection.
    (B) NO FIXED RELEASE PUBLISHED — the CVE genuinely applies and there is
        real residual risk. These are ACCEPTED RISK, not dismissals, and each
        one names the version that will fix it. Treat category (B) as an open
        security item on the project, not as closed paperwork.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

